  "@type": "sc:Dataset",
  "name": "DelegationCheck",
  "description": "DelegationCheck tests when an AI agent should check with the user before taking an action. Each of its 156 English scenarios gives a model a user instruction, some context, and one proposed tool call, and the model chooses to act, ask for permission, ask for a missing fact, or decline. Most scenarios come in 48 matched pairs that differ in one feature: scope, stakes, reversibility, or visibility to others. The release also contains the majority labels and vote counts of three annotators and the categorical decisions of ten models.",
  "conformsTo": [
    "http://mlcommons.org/croissant/1.0",
    "http://mlcommons.org/croissant/RAI/1.0"
  ],
  "url": "https://github.com/PieLord757/delegation-check",
  "sameAs": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05532",
  "license": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-04",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isLiveDataset": false,
  "keywords": [
    "AI agents",
    "tool use",
    "authorization",
    "human oversight",
    "benchmark",
    "matched pairs"
  ],
  "creator": {
    "@type": "sc:Person",
    "name": "Shiva Pochampally"
  },
  "citeAs": "@misc{pochampally2026delegationcheck, author = {Pochampally, Shiva}, title = {DelegationCheck: Measuring When AI Agents Should Ask Before Acting}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2610.05532}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05532}}",
  "rai:dataCollection": "The author wrote the 156 scenarios. Most are matched pairs that keep the instruction and proposed action fixed and change one sentence of context. Three annotators labeled every scenario independently. Ten models were evaluated with five trials per scenario; their responses were parsed into one of four decisions.",
  "rai:dataCollectionType": [
    "Manual Human Curation",
    "Experiments"
  ],
  "rai:dataAnnotationProtocol": "Three unpaid computer-science students with AI experience, not involved in building the benchmark, each labeled all 156 scenarios in their own random order through the same interface, with written definitions of the four decisions. They did not see model outputs or the authoring expectations. The reference label is the 2-of-3 or 3-of-3 majority (149 scenarios); disagreements were not adjudicated.",
  "rai:annotatorDemographics": "Three computer-science students with AI experience. No other demographic information was collected or released.",
  "rai:dataAnnotationAnalysis": "Krippendorff's alpha across the three annotators is .437 for the four-way label and .543 for act versus any other response. Seven scenarios have no majority.",
  "rai:dataPreprocessingProtocol": "Model responses were parsed into ACT, ASK_AUTHORIZATION, REQUEST_INFORMATION, REFUSE, or INVALID. Unparseable outputs were kept as INVALID and never rerun. Only these categorical results are released.",
  "rai:dataLimitations": "Synthetic, English-only scenarios in six task domains, each built around one proposed action rather than a full workflow. The annotators are three technically trained students and are not representative of users; their labels are a reference point, not ground truth. REQUEST_INFORMATION and REFUSE are sparse in the majority labels (8 and 1). Each matched-pair feature is built from three scenario families.",
  "rai:dataBiases": "Scenarios reflect the author's choice of domains and situations. The annotators share a technical background, which may make them more willing to let an agent act than a general user population.",
  "rai:personalSensitiveInformation": "None. Scenarios are synthetic and contain no personal data. Annotator identities, notes, and confidence ratings are withheld, and vote counts are released without a link to any person.",
  "rai:dataUseCases": "Intended: measuring and comparing how AI agents decide whether to act, ask, request information, or refuse, including pair responsiveness and judging versus acting. Not intended: certifying a model as safe to deploy, or training models (the release includes a contamination canary).",
  "rai:dataSocialImpact": "The benchmark is meant to help developers see when agents act without permission or ask too often. Scores on a public benchmark can be overfit, so results should be reported with the breakdowns in the paper.",
  "rai:dataReleaseMaintenancePlan": "Maintained by the author on GitHub. The v1.0 file is never edited in place; corrections or extensions are released as separately versioned files. Report problems through GitHub issues.",
